\textbf{Code-heavy business filler produced lower accuracy than unrelated prose.} The matched difference was \qualityTwoRelevantChange{} points [\qualityTwoRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityTwoRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the shortest tier and \qualityFourRelevantChange{} points [\qualityFourRelevantChangeLow{}, \qualityFourRelevantChangeHigh{}] over \qualityFourRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the middle tier. The respective paired outcomes were \qualityTwoRelevantWins{} win, \qualityTwoRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityTwoRelevantTies{} ties, then \qualityFourRelevantWins{} win, \qualityFourRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityFourRelevantTies{} ties. The longest-tier estimate was \qualityEightRelevantChange{} points, but its \qualityEightRelevantFamilyCount{} families are too sparse to strengthen that conclusion. Because the business filler also adds code-shaped candidates and repeated entity templates, these results do not show that topical relevance caused the difference.

